--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                 MYO Open Source Distribution User's Guide
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Thank you for your interest in the Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) product, and the MYO 
Open Source Distribution.  MYO stands for Mine, Yours, Ours and is a library and
Application Programming Interface to support virtual shared memory between 
processes on a host process and Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) coprocessor cards.  This 
package is supplementary to other Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) hardware and software, 
and is intended for researchers and advanced users.  

Notes: 
   o Before MYO source is used, Intel(R) MPSS SDK and the Intel Composer XE 
     Compatibility RPM should be installed. 
     In addition to drivers and tools, these packages contain:
	- Binary copies of the MYO libraries.
	- Header files for building application programs that use MYO. 

   o In this document, lines preceded by "user_prompt>" are used to represent a 
     Linux* shell prompt; text following this string on the same line represents
     commands to be executed in a Linux* shell.

   o In this documentation, Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) architecture is sometimes 
     referred to as MIC, which is an acronym for Many Integrated Core.  This 
     acronym is handy when used in file and directory names.

*Other names and brands may be claimed as the property of others.

1) System requirements

   o Supported hardware platform with at least one Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) 
     coprocessor installed.
   o Approved Linux* host operating system. Supported versions:  
	- Red Hat* Enterprise Linux* 64-bit 6.0 kernel 2.6.32-71
	- Red Hat* Enterprise Linux* 64-bit 6.1 kernel 2.6.32-131
	- Red Hat* Enterprise Linux* 64-bit 6.2 kernel 2.6.32-220
	- Red Hat* Enterprise Linux* 64-bit 6.3 kernel 2.6.32-279
	- SUSE* Linux* Enterprise Server SLES 11 SP1 kernel 2.6.32.12-0.7-default
	- SUSE* Linux* Enterprise Server SLES 11 SP2 kernel 3.0.13-0.27-default	   
   o Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) Linux* Driver Package (including Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM)
     MYO Library Binaries).
   o gcc compiler with Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) coprocessor support (installed with
     Intel(R) Xeon Phi(TM) Driver Package).
   o Intel(R) Composer XE 2013.

2) Extract MYO Open Source Distribution

   MYO source is delivered in the file myo-[version number].tar.bz2. 
   
   In the tar file, files are a tree relative to the myo-[version number] 
   directory.  Extract the archive to the desired directory with the following 
   steps.
  
      user_prompt> cd [directory of choice]
      user_prompt> tar -xf myo-[version number].tar.bz2
      user_prompt> cd myo-[version number]
 
3) Verify extraction
  
   After MYO is extracted, the myo directory tree will be similar to the 
   following:
  
   myo-[version number]
   |-- include         Contains header files used in the build of applications 
   |                   and tutorials.
   |-- src             Contains source files of MYO runtime library.
   |-- src/include     Contains header files used in the build of the library.
   |-- tools           Contains the environment setting configuration files.
   |-- docs            Contains myo reference documents.
   `-- docs/tutorials  Contains tutorials that demonstrate how to program with 
                       MYO.
  
   Drilling down further into the src directories, a map of where features are 
   implemented is as follows:
  
   |-- allocator       Contains the memory management module (Memory allocator).
   |-- communication   Contains the implementation of the communication.
   |-- consistent      Contains the implementation of VSM protocol.
   |-- include         Contains all internal header files.
   |-- machinedep      Contains the platform dependent portion of MYO.
   |-- misc            Contains all other files.
   |-- Makefile        Makefile for MYO libraries.
   |-- pinnedmem       Contains the implementation of get pinned memory.
   |-- rfunc           Contains the implementation of APIs related with remote 
   |                   function call.
   |-- svar            Contains the implementation of APIs related with shared 
   |                   variables.
   `-- sync            Contains global (cross-bus) sync operations (mutex, 
                       barrier and semaphore).

4) Build the Myo and Myo DBL Libraries

   You may build the myo and myo dbl libraries from the myo-[version number]/src 
   directory** using the file myo-[version number]/src/Makefile with gnu make.
   
      [From myo-[version number]/src directory**]
      user_prompt> make TARGET_ICC='icc -mmic'
   
   This will create a myo-[version number]/bin directory** and the following 
   dynamic libraries:
   
	libmyo-client.so.0     libmyodbl-service.so.0
	libmyodbl-client.so.0  libmyo-service.so.0

   To remove the files created during the build, enter the command:
   
      user_prompt> make clean 
   
5) Tutorials

   A great way to test changes to the MYO libraries is to run them with the 
   tutorials that are located in myo-[version number]/docs/tutorials. 
   myo-[version number]/docs/tutorials/README describes the details of building 
   and running the tutorials, and we attempt not to duplicate the same 
   information in this file.  However, there are a few extra steps you will take 
   because you are building the myo library before you begin working with the 
   tutorials.
   
   By default, the tutorials run with the MYO libraries that are installed with 
   the driver.  When each tutorial is run, it will copy the MYO libraries from 
   their installed location.  By copying the libraries each time, the tutorials 
   insure that the latest library is used.  In addition, because the card is 
   often configured to run from a RAM disk, when the library is copied to the 
   card as the tutorials are run, it insures that there has been a library 
   loaded since the last power cycle of the card.

   To insure your libraries are used, you must replace the myo libraries that 
   were installed with Intel(R) MPSS with the libraries that you built.
   
6) Additional documentation

   You can get additional documentation about the myo API and its source code 
   from the myo-[version number]/docs directory.  This source archive also 
   generates man files that can be revised and viewed as part of your changes to 
   the MYO libraries.
